# modules.cpp Linux platform specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented native hosted Linux target lane and its
platform providers. docs/modules-platforms.mdy owns SDK selection and provider
injection; this document owns the Linux mappings from portable interfaces to
libc and Linux UAPI operations.

# Lane

linux-aarch64 and linux-x86_64 are hosted SDKs without runtime prefixes.
Configure selects one with --target-host and may select runner native. The
native runner is accepted only when the target triple equals the build machine
compiler's target triple, then executes the image through /usr/bin/env with
application arguments forwarded, which is why a lane can only be built and run
on the architecture it names.

Each SDK is complete without a board. Two boards exist per architecture and
neither supplies sources, a linker script, or a bare-metal responsibility:
generic-linux-<arch> names its own map provider, and sdl-linux-<arch> derives
from it and moves the screen and pointer into a window. A hosted board is what
the loader accepts here rather than the bare-metal shape, and the processor
registry carries a row per hosted triple whose argument list is empty, because a
native lane wants no processor arguments at all.

# Device map

On a microcontroller the board is the wiring: the Pico provider knows which
pad is ADC channel zero because the silicon says so. On Linux the same
portable calls -- gpio_configure(3), spi_write(0, ...), adc_read(1, ...) --
land on kernel devices whose names are facts about one machine: a gpiochip
and a line offset, a spidev path, an I2C adapter number, an IIO device, a
pwmchip. The device map is the one place those facts live, so that no
provider hard-codes a path and no application names one.

platform.linux.map is a platform interface, portable across Linux boards
rather than across platforms. The selected SDK binds
platform.linux.defaults, and each device provider depends on the interface so
fixed-point provider injection selects that one map provider. The map has
two layers:

- Compiled defaults from the selected board. The SDK's default provider
  registers an empty map, nothing wired. Each generic board binds its own
  provider -- platform.linux.generic_aarch64.map registers a map whose one
  fact is board.name -- and a board with real wiring registers a fuller
  default, by the same static registration every provider uses.
- An override file named by MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP, applied over the defaults.
  Omitted fields retain compiled defaults.

The first provider call resolves the map: the defaults are copied, the
override applied if the variable is set, the result validated and cached for
the process. Resolution happens once; a later change to the file changes
nothing until the program is started again. A map that fails to parse or
validate leaves board() empty and makes every provider operation answer
BadArgument, and the resolution carries the fault: the file, the line, the
offending key, the reason, and for a duplicate the earlier line as well.

## Override grammar

The override is one `facility.instance.field = value` assignment per line.
Whitespace and blank lines are ignored, # begins a comment outside quotes,
integers are decimal or hexadecimal, booleans are yes/no, and strings are
double quoted with only backslash and quote escapes. A selector is `auto`,
`index:K`, or a quoted name. A duplicate key names both lines; an unknown
key, malformed value, or named missing file is an error. So is a path that
is not a regular file, or one larger than 64 KiB: the first board query is
what reads the override, and it must return. The path is opened without
blocking, the descriptor itself is checked to be a regular file, and at most
the limit is read, so a FIFO put in the file's place cannot hold the open
and a file that grows while it is read cannot hold the read.

## Keys

The facility number in a key -- the N of gpio.N -- is the number the
portable interface accepts: gpio.3 is the GPIO that gpio_configure(3)
configures, spi.0 is SPI instance zero. Numbers are dense from zero; naming
gpio.5 alone declares six GPIOs, the first five with no chip, which
validation refuses.

Opening fence
board.name = "..."                 # what mm::mcu::board() reports
board.led.name = "..."             # optional; default LED
board.led.gpio = G                 # a declared GPIO
board.led.active-high = yes|no     # default yes

gpio.N.chip = "/dev/gpiochipK"     # required
gpio.N.offset = L                  # the line on that chip
gpio.N.name = "..."                # optional; default gpio.N; unique

spi.N.path = "/dev/spidevB.C"      # opened with SPI_NO_CS
spi.N.clock-gpio = G               # the wiring a caller must repeat
spi.N.transmit-gpio = G            # in its SpiConfiguration
spi.N.receive-gpio = G             # optional
spi.N.max-speed = HZ               # a caller's baud may not exceed it
spi.N.speed-fixed = yes|no         # yes: the baud must equal max-speed
spi.N.mode = mode0..mode3
spi.N.bit-order = msb|lsb

i2c.N.adapter = K                  # /dev/i2c-K
i2c.N.data-gpio = G                # the wiring a caller must repeat
i2c.N.clock-gpio = G
i2c.N.baud = HZ                    # default 400000
i2c.N.baud-fixed = yes|no          # default yes: the caller's baud must
                                   # equal it; no answers Unsupported,
                                   # since i2c-dev cannot set a rate

uart.N.path = "/dev/ttyXXX"
uart.N.baud = HZ                   # default 115200; a termios rate
uart.N.parity = none|even|odd
uart.N.data-bits = 5..8            # default 8
uart.N.stop-bits = 1|2             # default 1
uart.N.write-deadline-ms = MS      # default 500; a write gives up after it

rtc.path = "/dev/rtcK"             # empty: CLOCK_REALTIME stands in
rtc.convention = utc|local         # what the RTC keeps; default utc
rtc.allow-write = yes|no           # default no; with no path, a write of
                                   # the system clock is refused

display.card = SELECTOR            # the DRM card, connector, and mode
display.connector = SELECTOR
display.mode = SELECTOR
display.width = W                  # optional; default the mode's
display.height = H

touch.device = SELECTOR            # the evdev device
touch.invert-x = yes|no
touch.invert-y = yes|no
touch.swap-axes = yes|no

imu.device = SELECTOR              # the IIO device and its trigger
imu.trigger = SELECTOR
imu.timeout-ms = MS                # default 500; nonzero

adc.device = SELECTOR              # the IIO device the channels are on
adc.N.channel = C                  # in_voltage<C>_raw
adc.N.gpio = G                     # optional; a declared GPIO, once
adc.N.name = "..."                 # optional; default adc.N; unique
adc.N.bits = B                     # default 12; [1, 31]
adc.N.reference-mv = V             # optional; else from the IIO scale

pwm.N.chip = K                     # /sys/class/pwm/pwmchipK
pwm.N.channel = C                  # pwmC under it
pwm.N.gpio = G                     # optional; a declared GPIO, once
pwm.N.name = "..."                 # optional; default pwm.N; unique
pwm.N.group = Q                    # optional; a known shared counter
Closing fence

Validation, after every line has applied: every GPIO has a chip and a name
unique among GPIOs; the LED, and any analog entry's pad, names a declared
GPIO, and a pad is attached to at most one channel and at most one output;
names are unique within each analog inventory; adc.N.bits is in [1, 31],
because sysfs does not publish a raw channel's width and the map must say
it. The SPI and I2C wiring fields exist so that a caller's configuration is
checked against the board rather than trusted: a configuration naming other
pins is BadArgument, as it would be on a microcontroller whose mux does not
route the bus there.

## An example

A Raspberry Pi with an SPI e-paper on CE0, a PCF85063 clock on I2C 1, an
ADS1015 converter on the same bus exposed through IIO, and a hardware PWM
on GPIO 18:

Opening fence
# MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP=/etc/modules.cpp/pi.map
board.name = "pi-bench"
gpio.0.chip = "/dev/gpiochip0"
gpio.0.offset = 17                 # e-paper data/command
gpio.1.chip = "/dev/gpiochip0"
gpio.1.offset = 27                 # e-paper reset
gpio.2.chip = "/dev/gpiochip0"
gpio.2.offset = 24                 # e-paper busy
gpio.3.chip = "/dev/gpiochip0"
gpio.3.offset = 18
gpio.3.name = "pwm0"
spi.0.path = "/dev/spidev0.0"
spi.0.clock-gpio = 11
spi.0.transmit-gpio = 10
spi.0.max-speed = 4000000
spi.0.mode = mode0
i2c.0.adapter = 1
i2c.0.data-gpio = 2
i2c.0.clock-gpio = 3
adc.device = "ads1015"
adc.0.channel = 0
adc.0.bits = 11
pwm.0.chip = 0
pwm.0.channel = 0
pwm.0.gpio = 3
Closing fence

The SPI and I2C GPIO numbers here are Broadcom pin numbers the caller's
configuration must repeat; they are checked, not driven, since spidev and
i2c-dev own the pins. The e-paper's three control lines are GPIOs 0 to 2 of
this map, the numbers a display driver's Wiring would name, and the PWM's
pad is GPIO 3 so that pwm_output_for_gpio(3) finds output 0.

# Providers

- platform.linux.stdio uses descriptors zero and one with zero-timeout input
  polling, partial output counts, ignored SIGPIPE, and separate sticky input and
  output loss. A write must not wait on a reader or a terminal, and the
  inherited descriptor shares its blocking flag with the shell that started
  the program, so the provider does not change it. What a write does depends
  on what descriptor one is, checked on every call so a redirection is
  followed:
  - a pipe, a FIFO, or a terminal is reopened through /proc as a private
    nonblocking descriptor of the provider's own; a write there returns
    what fitted, and the call gives up after one second with what it
    managed, recomputing the time left after every interruption;
  - a socket is written with MSG_DONTWAIT under the same one-second bound;
  - a regular file is written plainly on the inherited descriptor, with no
    bound: it waits on the file system and on no one else, and a write
    there is done when it returns, so the console contract's partial count
    arises only from a short write the file system itself made;
  - anything else, or a reopen that fails for a reason other than a missing
    reader, is refused with TransportError for that call and nothing is
    recorded, so a later call may succeed; a reopen that fails because the
    pipe has no reader records output as lost, as a broken pipe does.
  Reopening through /proc can be denied, so an inherited pipe or terminal
  that is writable may still be refused here; the provider prefers a refused
  write to one it cannot bound. On the bounded paths the console contract's
  partial count is how the caller learns that the deadline, not the data,
  ended the call.
- platform.linux.mcu uses GPIO-v2 line ioctls, spidev with SPI_NO_CS, I2C_RDWR,
  nonblocking termios writes with a deadline, and CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Its ADC
  reads IIO sysfs -- in_voltage<C>_raw for the count, in_voltage<C>_scale or
  the device-wide in_voltage_scale for millivolts per count, parsed as a
  decimal into nanovolts without floating point, so a channel's reference is
  the scale times its full-scale count unless the map states one; a negative
  or out-of-range raw value is TransportError. Its PWM drives
  /sys/class/pwm: configure exports the channel unless the directory already
  exists, in which case the output is someone else's and answers Busy, waits
  a bounded half second for the attributes, writes duty_cycle zero, period,
  and enable one in that order, and reads the period back as the actual; a
  failed step disables and unexports only its own claim. Both period limits
  are reported zero, since sysfs publishes none, and a period the kernel
  refuses with EINVAL is BadArgument. A map entry without a group is its own.
  The provider keeps one owner per pad across GPIO, the edge latch, ADC, and
  PWM, as docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy's pad-ownership section says: an
  analog claim closes a plain GPIO line request and is refused on a watched
  one; a pinmux the kernel or firmware changed outside the process is not in
  that record and surfaces as the kernel's answer.
- platform.linux.rtc uses RTC_RD_TIME, RTC_SET_TIME, RTC_VL_READ and RTC_VL_CLR,
  or CLOCK_REALTIME when no RTC path is configured.
- platform.linux.display uses legacy DRM/KMS dumb buffers. It publishes RGB565
  only, keeps writes in a shadow buffer until Full refresh, and refuses Partial.
- platform.linux.touch decodes type-B multitouch, legacy absolute touch, and
  relative pointers. Type-A multitouch is refused.
- platform.linux.imu obtains all six axes from one IIO buffered record. It
  derives layout from scan types and scale from realbits plus the IIO scale.

The MCU provider watches GPIO edges on its existing line request, changing
configuration in place, or requests a new line when none exists. A watched
descriptor is nonblocking. Watch discards queued setup events before it
returns; take reads at most sixteen records per call, and wait polls against a
monotonic deadline. A fresh request asks for a sixty-four-event queue, which
the kernel may cap. Unwatch closes the request and leaves the pin unconfigured.
Linux GPIO cdev delivers readiness through poll; no signal handler or waiter
thread is installed for this facility.
An edge request refused with ENXIO or EOPNOTSUPP answers Unsupported;
those errnos retain their transport-error meaning for other MCU operations.

No provider silently maps a permission failure to Unsupported. No provider the
SDK binds adds a third-party library or an explicit link input: every one of
them is written against libc and the Linux userspace kernel ABI, whose headers
come from packages any build here already needs.

# SDL2 backend

platform.linux.sdl implements mm.display and mm.touch over SDL2. It exists for
the one thing the DRM provider cannot do: run in a window. DRM needs DRM master,
a compositor holds it, and no provider can take it from a live holder, so
anything visual on the DRM lane means leaving the session for a virtual
terminal.

One module implements both interfaces because SDL has one video subsystem, one
window, one event queue, and state shared between drawing and input; two modules
could not own that between them. The board binds the same module twice, and an
application reaching both interfaces still links one of its objects.

It is the platform's only optional package, and the only provider here that
names a library. platforms/linux/sdl2 is that library: a kind: library whose
source records an installed SDL2 rather than vendoring one, declaring an
interface of a single link-input and no include directory, because SDL2's
headers resolve from a default search directory without one. The provider is a
folder inside it, as platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk arranges its own.

Selection is what keeps the package optional. The SDKs bind the DRM and evdev
providers; only an sdl-linux-<arch> board replaces those two bindings, so a
lane that does not name one links no SDL2 and needs none installed. An
absent package is not an absent checkout, because the checkout is a record, so
nothing notices it before the compiler does.

The provider publishes RGB565 and converts to SDL's native-endian sixteen-bit
format on the way to the texture. The two disagree by byte order on a
little-endian host and nothing reports it: feeding mm.display's red, 0xf8 then
0x00, to an unswapped surface draws blue. It keeps writes in a shadow buffer
until Full refresh and refuses Partial, as the DRM provider does. Touch reports
the pointer while the left button is held and answers Unsupported when no window
exists, rather than opening a second one.

# Qualification

Machine-independent parser, status, conversion, and decoder behavior belongs in
unit tests. Device qualification depends on the running machine: absent devices
are Unsupported, explicit stale paths are BadArgument, permission failures are
TransportError, and held devices are Busy. linux-smoke exits non-zero only when
a present facility fails.

The SDL lane inverts that expectation and is the only one that does: a window
needs nothing of the session, so an application there is expected to succeed on
an ordinary desktop and a failure is a failure. scripts/build-linux-sdl.sh gates
its --run accordingly, while the three DRM-backed scripts report and do not.
